## Rules

- Before building anything that depends on an external system or real data, check the
  request against the live source (API, database or URL) and stop if the premise is false.
- Write failing tests before implementing. Never weaken, skip or delete a test to get green.
- Never push, merge, deploy, apply a migration, run deployment or database tools against
  production, or read .env* unless I ask.
- Verify library and tool behaviour from the installed code or version-pinned docs, not
  from memory.
- Tell me what's wrong with a request before building it, and give a recommendation, not a
  survey.
- Report faithfully: show failing output, name skipped steps, and don't call partial work
  done.
- No secrets in source. A new env var also goes in `.env.example`, and you tell me about it.
- Branch before committing on the default branch. Commit subjects are imperative, under 72
  characters; the body says why.
- End every task with a one-line status of where things stand.

## Workflow

- New product or project idea: use the start-product skill.
- Any feature, fix or change in an existing project, including "next": use the
  ship-feature skill.
- Edits that can't change behaviour (typos, comments, docs wording, formatting): make them
  directly on a branch, without ship-feature. If unsure whether behaviour could change, use
  ship-feature.
- Never merge, push or deploy, even at the end of a skill.
